  "resource": "obr_savings_income_growth_facts.json",
  "purpose": "OBR Economic and fiscal outlook, October 2026, receipts Table 4.5 (\"Self-employed income streams: growth rates\"): the savings income row's growth on a year earlier, pre-measures, from 2024-25 to 2031-32. The SPI Table 3.7 interest and other income rows by band grow by it from 2023-24 to the calibration year's window, the path the Budget's savings costing grows its base by (Mar\u00eda's ruling of 2026-10-29, microcosm#1418). Scope: the row is the savings income of self assessment filers, the line the OBR forecasts self assessment receipts on, where the Table 3.7 rows count every taxpayer, most of whom do not file, so part of its +35.0% in 2024-25 may be savers entering self assessment as interest passed the Personal Savings Allowance, a change in who files. ONS households' interest received (D.41, HAXV, 30 September 2026) grows x1.048 from FY2023-24 to calendar 2025 (GBP 91.1bn to GBP 95.5bn) against x1.356 on this path; HAXV is after the FISIM adjustment and counts ISA interest and non-taxpayers, so it is not like for like (Vahid's review of microcosm#1420). The engine projects interest at GDP per head until PolicyEngine/policyengine-uk#2638 lands, so the solve reaches this level only by moving weight toward savers, which the next national's weight check on the records that carry interest measures.",
